.TH RANDOM 7 "31 October 2026" "random"
.SH NAME
random, urandom \- random numbers from the kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/random
.br
.B /dev/urandom
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reading either device gives random bytes, as many as asked for.
Programs that need random numbers for keys, salts, temporary names or
seeds read them here; Python's
.B os.urandom
and
.B secrets
do.
.PP
The two devices are the same generator and behave the same: neither ever
blocks, and there is no count of entropy.
.B /dev/random
is there for programs that open it by that name.
.PP
The generator is ChaCha20 (RFC 8439) with a 256-bit key.
Every read ends by replacing the key with output no one sees, so a later
state of the kernel tells nothing about bytes read before; the key also
changes every 64 KiB within one long read.
.PP
What is written to either device is mixed into the key, by anyone; it
can only add to what the generator knows, never take away.
The kernel also mixes in, at load, at every open and at every read, the
clock ticks since boot, the beam position of the chipset and the timers
of both CIAs.
After a boot those are guessable, so the boot step
.BR randseed (1M)
writes in the seed saved at the last shutdown, the date and the process
table before anything else uses the devices.
.PP
The driver is the loadable module
.BR random ,
of the demand class: it loads at the first open of either device, and is
never unloaded, since that would lose what was written in.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/random
character major 48, minor 0.
.TP
.B /dev/urandom
character major 48, minor 1.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/random
the module.
.TP
.B /etc/random-seed
the seed kept across reboots, 512 bytes, readable by root only.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Another minor number gives
.BR ENXIO .
The devices have no
.B ioctl
and cannot be mapped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.ft CW
dd if=/dev/urandom bs=16 count=1 | od -x
echo something only I know > /dev/urandom
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR randseed (1M)
.SH NOTES
The interface (two minors that do not block, writes that mix in, a seed
file across reboots) is that of isoriano's random-amix, the driver that
first gave Amix these devices.
